{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["The reason for <b>And I will surely hide My face</b> — yet another time — is that because Israel reflected in their hearts that they had sinned against Elohim, and because their Elohim was not in their midst these evils found them, it would have been fitting, given the abundance of HaShem's lovingkindness, to help them and deliver them, for they had already repented of idolatry, as it says (Jeremiah 2:35), 'Behold, I will enter into judgment with you because you say, I have not sinned.' Therefore He said that on account of all the great evil they did in trusting in idolatry, He would hide His face from them yet again — not like the first hiding of the face, when He hid the face of His compassion and many evils and troubles found them; rather, they would be in the hiding of the face of redemption yet stand with the assurance of the face of His compassion, (Leviticus 26:44) 'And yet for all that, when they are in the land of their enemies, I will not reject them, neither will I abhor them,' and so forth — until they add to the aforementioned remorse a full confession and complete repentance, as mentioned above (Deuteronomy 30:2), 'And you shall return to HaShem your Elohim,' and so forth."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:57:31.204811552Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}